# Trading — Institutional Engineering Guardrails (Python)

This file defines how we design trading logic and what "good" looks like.

## 1) What We Trade vs How We Trade
- What: strategy models produce targets/intents based on market + state.
- How: execution layer translates those intents into venue-specific actions.

Keep these separate.

## 2) Canonical Models (Required)
Use typed models for:
- Market snapshot / book (mid, spread, levels, seq, ts)
- OrderIntent: side, qty, limit_price, tif, flags (post_only)
- Order: internal state + venue ids
- Fill: price, qty, fee, ts
- Position: net, avg price, realized/unrealized
- RiskResult: allowed + reason codes + computed exposures

No dicts across boundaries.

## 3) Pricing & Order Policy Defaults (Safe)
- Default to limit orders (not market).
- Apply price bands:
  - if buy: limit_price <= mid * (1 + max_buy_slippage)
  - if sell: limit_price >= mid * (1 - max_sell_slippage)
- Default to small size unless explicitly configured.
- Always include a time-in-force policy (GTC/IOC/etc.) appropriate to venue.
- Avoid aggressive cancels; enforce cancel throttles.

## 4) Risk Policy Reason Codes (Standardize)
Examples:
- RISK_MAX_POSITION
- RISK_MAX_NOTIONAL
- RISK_ORDER_TOO_LARGE
- RISK_PRICE_OUT_OF_BOUNDS
- RISK_DATA_STALE
- RISK_RATE_LIMIT
- RISK_KILL_SWITCH
- RISK_RECONCILE_DIVERGENCE

Risk checks must emit:
- allowed/denied
- reason codes
- key inputs (mid, qty, projected position, limits version)

## 5) Execution Tactics (Keep Simple)
Phase 1:
- One order per intent.
- Optional: split large intent into N child orders (deterministic).
- Optional: passive-first (post-only) then relax if not filled.

Phase 2:
- Child order management, pegging, smart routing (only if needed).

## 6) Failure Modes (Always Handle Explicitly)
- adapter disconnect
- websocket gaps
- partial acks
- duplicate messages
- venue rejects
- fills after cancel request (race)
- replay/restart with in-flight orders

Rule:
- never "assume" success; always confirm via user stream or REST reconcile.

## 7) Config & Change Control
- Limits and strategy params must be validated and versioned.
- Emit ConfigLoaded event with config hash + version.
- Any config change must be auditable.

## 8) “No Surprise Orders”
- Default execution_enabled = false.
- Provide a dry-run mode that produces intents but never submits.
- Execution requires an ExecutionPermit object obtained only after health/risk initialization.
